# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PULSAR — Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NOTHING here is required just to see the site — the home page shows
# fresh music from the free Apple feed with zero config. These enable
# the optional persistence + curation layers.

# ── DSP playlist creation (the "Create playlist" feature) ─────
# Each configured service builds a REAL playlist on the user's account when
# they export a crate. Any service left unconfigured falls back to CSV + copy.
# All redirect URIs must exactly match your site origin + "/"
#   e.g. https://pulsar-ten-sigma.vercel.app/  (and http://127.0.0.1:3000/ local)
#
# NOTE: these values are ALSO served at runtime by /api/dsp-config, so setting
# them in Vercel takes effect on the next page load — no redeploy required.
# The NEXT_PUBLIC_* names still work (inlined at build); the plain names are
# read live by the server route.
#
# Spotify — Authorization Code + PKCE, no secret. Create an app at
#   https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard, add the redirect URI, copy the id.
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
#
# YouTube Music — Google OAuth (Authorization Code + PKCE) + YouTube Data API v3.
# NOTE: this needs BOTH variables. Google deprecated the implicit flow, so the
# authorization code must be exchanged server-side (by /api/youtube-token),
# which requires the client secret. The secret is server-only and never ships
# to the browser.
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com → create/select a project
#   2. APIs & Services → Library → enable "YouTube Data API v3"
#   3. APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen → External. Add the scope
#      https://www.googleapis.com/auth/youtube and add yourself under
#      "Test users" (that scope is SENSITIVE, so until Google verifies the app
#      only listed test users can connect — up to 100 — and they'll see an
#      "unverified app" warning they must click through).
#   4. Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application
#        Authorised JavaScript origins:  https://your-site.vercel.app
#        Authorised redirect URIs:       https://your-site.vercel.app/
#      (add http://localhost:3000 and http://localhost:3000/ for local dev)
#   5. Paste the Client ID below, then REDEPLOY — NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are
#      inlined at build time, so setting the var alone changes nothing.
#
#   ⚠ Quota: the YouTube Data API bills 100 units per search + 50 per insert,
#   against a default 10,000 units/day for the whole project — roughly 65
#   tracks per DAY across all users combined. Fine for testing; request a quota
#   increase in the Cloud console before putting this in front of real users.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# ── YOUTUBE_API_KEY — makes the samples feature play video ──────────────
#
# This is a SERVER-SIDE API KEY, and it is separate from the OAuth client
# above. The OAuth client acts on behalf of a signed-in user (needed to
# create playlists in their account); this key acts on behalf of the app and
# is only used to look up a video id for "<artist> <title>".
#
# Without it, /api/ytvideo returns { videoId: null, reason: "no-key" } for
# anything not in the curated map, and the sample cards fall back to a
# "Find it on YouTube" search link instead of an inline player. That is a
# working experience, just not an embedded one.
#
# Set it up (5 minutes, free):
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com → pick the SAME project you used for
#      the OAuth client above.
#   2. APIs & Services → Library → search "YouTube Data API v3" → Enable.
#      (Already enabled if YouTube playlist export works — skip.)
#   3. APIs & Services → Credentials → + Create credentials → API key.
#   4. Click the new key's name to edit it, then:
#        Application restrictions → None. This key is used from the SERVER,
#          so an HTTP-referrer restriction would block it. Do not pick
#          "Websites" — that is for browser keys and will 403 every call.
#        API restrictions → Restrict key → tick ONLY "YouTube Data API v3".
#      Save.
#   5. Paste it into Vercel → Settings → Environment Variables as
#      YOUTUBE_API_KEY (all environments), then redeploy.
#
# NEVER prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that inlines it into the client
# bundle where anyone can read and burn your quota.
#
#   ⚠ Quota: a search costs 100 units against the project's 10,000/day, so
#   ~100 lookups a day shared with playlist export (which spends from the
#   same pool). Results are cached for 30 days per artist+title, so real
#   traffic re-uses them; a cold catalogue crawl would exhaust it. Request an
#   increase in the Cloud console before heavy public use.
YOUTUBE_API_KEY=
#
# Apple Music — MusicKit JS. Requires a PAID Apple Developer membership.
#   Create a MusicKit key at https://developer.apple.com/account (Keys), then:
NEXT_PUBLIC_APPLE_MUSIC_ENABLED=false
APPLE_TEAM_ID=            # 10-char Team ID
APPLE_KEY_ID=            # the MusicKit key's Key ID
APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=       # the .p8 contents (PEM; literal \n newlines are fine)
#
# Tidal — this note used to say Tidal's write access was gated. That is now out
# of date: TIDAL runs a public developer platform at openapi.tidal.com/v2 with
# OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code + PKCE and a `playlists.write` scope — the same
# flow this codebase already implements for Spotify, so a Tidal provider is the
# cheapest remaining native export to add. The one catch is that Open API access
# is approved per client id rather than issued instantly.
#   https://developer.tidal.com/documentation
#
# SoundCloud's API remains closed to new applications.
#
# Deezer is NOT a candidate, despite the feed already reading its public catalog
# API without a key. Those are two different things: the keyless search endpoints
# are open, but the authenticated user API — the one that could create a playlist
# — no longer issues tokens to new applications. Deezer's OAuth also predates
# PKCE and needs a client secret exchanged server-side.
#
# Until a provider is implemented, anything unconfigured falls back to CSV + copy.

# ── Supabase (for the growing archive) ───────────────────────
# Get from: https://app.supabase.com → Project Settings → API
# Needed by `npm run ingest` and the daily GitHub Action.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ...

# ── Open-model curation via Ollama (optional) ────────────────
# The ingest job asks this model for a curator note + mood per release.
# Local Ollama (default):
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.1
# Ollama Cloud instead of local (what the daily Vercel/GitHub job should use —
# a local Ollama is unreachable from serverless). Point at your cloud endpoint,
# your API key, and the model name:
#   OLLAMA_BASE_URL=https://ollama.com
#   OLLAMA_API_KEY=<your-ollama-cloud-key>     # keep SECRET — never commit
#   OLLAMA_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash:0731-cloud
#
# Where to set it so enrichment actually runs:
#   • GitHub Actions daily ingest → repo Settings → Secrets (OLLAMA_BASE_URL,
#     OLLAMA_API_KEY, OLLAMA_MODEL) — the workflow already passes them through.
#   • Vercel cron → Project → Settings → Environment Variables (server-side).
# Tuning:
# ENRICH_LIMIT=30          # how many newest releases to enrich per run
# ENRICH_CONCURRENCY=3     # parallel requests to Ollama

# ── Agent trigger (protects the /api/agent POST endpoint) ────
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
AGENT_TRIGGER_SECRET=your-secret-here

# ── Optional: Claude-powered discovery agent (npm run agent) ──
# Only if you want the web-browsing curator instead of the Apple feed.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=BSA...

# ── Local scheduler (optional) ───────────────────────────────
AGENT_CRON=0 8 * * *
AGENT_TIMEZONE=UTC
RUN_ON_START=false
